($bc40986$love-us-vibes-13$bc40986$,$bc40987$small_gap_both_content$bc40987$,$bc40988$Case dismissed. Settings synced.$bc40988$,$bc40989$$bc40989$,$bc40990$You're both running on nearly identical reassurance settings, which is rarer than it sounds, most couples have a gap here that turns into a recurring fight. You just happen to need the same amount of 'we're good' to actually believe it.$bc40990$,$bc40991$name the actual phrase that makes you feel reassured, just so it's on record. same, compare.$bc40991$,$bc40992$Case dismissed. Settings synced.$bc40992$,$bc40993$Approved v4 template$bc40993$,$bc40994$Shared Discovery$bc40994$,$bc40995$$bc40995$,$bc40996$name the actual phrase that makes you feel reassured, just so it's on record.$bc40996$,$bc40997$same, compare.$bc40997$,$bc40998$Each guess how many times the other actually needs to hear 'we're okay' after a rough moment. Compare, bet it's the same number.$bc40998$,$bc40999$Case dismissed. Settings synced.$bc40999$),
($bc41000$love-us-vibes-13$bc41000$,$bc41001$small_gap_split$bc41001$,$bc41002$Case dismissed. Alarm calibrated.$bc41002$,$bc41003$[A] = the partner who indicated it bothers them; [B] = the partner who indicated it doesn't.$bc41003$,$bc41004$[A]'s not needy, [A]'s just running a smoke detector that's a little more sensitive than [B]'s, same amount of actual smoke, different alarm threshold. [B]'s detector just hasn't gone off yet. Once the alarm's calibrated, reassurance stops feeling optional and starts being something you both know how to give.$bc41004$,$bc41005$say specifically what reassurance you need, instead of hoping it's noticed. offer it once, unprompted, next time something feels slightly off.$bc41005$,$bc41006$Case dismissed. Alarm calibrated.$bc41006$,$bc41007$Approved v4 template$bc41007$,$bc41008$Unexpected Analogy$bc41008$,$bc41009$[A] = the partner who indicated it bothers them; [B] = the partner who indicated it doesn't.$bc41009$,$bc41010$say specifically what reassurance you need, instead of hoping it's noticed.$bc41010$,$bc41011$offer it once, unprompted, next time something feels slightly off.$bc41011$,$bc41012$Agree on one specific phrase [A] can ask for directly when they need it, no guilt attached to asking.$bc41012$,$bc41013$Case dismissed. Alarm calibrated.$bc41013$),
($bc41014$love-us-vibes-13$bc41014$,$bc41015$small_gap_both_discontent$bc41015$,$bc41016$Case dismissed. Department now co-run.$bc41016$,$bc41017$$bc41017$,$bc41018$You're both quietly running the Reassurance Department solo, each assuming asking for it makes you 'too much,' when the amount you both need is basically identical. Trading the actual phrases turns two people quietly under-resourced into two people who finally know exactly what the other needs.$bc41018$,$bc41019$ask for reassurance directly this week, at least once, without pre-apologizing for needing it. same.$bc41019$,$bc41020$Case dismissed. Department now co-run.$bc41020$,$bc41021$Approved v4 template$bc41021$,$bc41022$Rename$bc41022$,$bc41023$$bc41023$,$bc41024$ask for reassurance directly this week, at least once, without pre-apologizing for needing it.$bc41024$,$bc41025$same.$bc41025$,$bc41026$Each write down the actual phrase that would make you feel reassured. Trade them. Use them on purpose this week.$bc41026$,$bc41027$Case dismissed. Department now co-run.$bc41027$),
($bc41028$love-us-vibes-13$bc41028$,$bc41029$large_gap_both_content$bc41029$,$bc41030$Case dismissed. Translation successful.$bc41030$,$bc41031$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance.$bc41031$,$bc41032$[A], you're not insecure, you're just running on a system that needs the words said out loud to fully land, while [B], your system runs on actions and assumes that's enough. Neither system is broken, you're just speaking different reassurance languages.$bc41032$,$bc41033$name one time [B]'s actions, not words, actually reassured you without you realizing it in the moment. say the actual words once anyway, just to see how it feels for both of you.$bc41033$,$bc41034$Case dismissed. Translation successful.$bc41034$,$bc41035$Approved v4 template$bc41035$,$bc41036$Rename$bc41036$,$bc41037$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance.$bc41037$,$bc41038$name one time [B]'s actions, not words, actually reassured you without you realizing it in the moment.$bc41038$,$bc41039$say the actual words once anyway, just to see how it feels for both of you.$bc41039$,$bc41040$[B] says the actual words once this week, unprompted, even though it doesn't feel necessary to them. Note how it lands for [A].$bc41040$,$bc41041$Case dismissed. Translation successful.$bc41041$),
($bc41042$love-us-vibes-13$bc41042$,$bc41043$large_gap_split$bc41043$,$bc41044$Case dismissed. Formula on file.$bc41044$,$bc41045$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance and is bothered by the gap.$bc41045$,$bc41046$This isn't [A] being insecure, it's that [A]'s reassurance needs have been running completely invisibly, [B] genuinely didn't know there was a system to run. You can't meet a need you don't know exists. Once the formula's on file, [A] stops having to hope it gets noticed and starts actually getting it.$bc41046$,$bc41047$name the actual moment you needed reassurance and didn't get it. offer it preemptively once this week, even before being asked.$bc41047$,$bc41048$Case dismissed. Formula on file.$bc41048$,$bc41049$Approved v4 template$bc41049$,$bc41050$Reframe$bc41050$,$bc41051$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance and is bothered by the gap.$bc41051$,$bc41052$name the actual moment you needed reassurance and didn't get it.$bc41052$,$bc41053$offer it preemptively once this week, even before being asked.$bc41053$,$bc41054$[A] explains exactly what reassurance sounds like when it actually works. Write it down together as the official formula.$bc41054$,$bc41055$Case dismissed. Formula on file.$bc41055$),
($bc41056$love-us-vibes-13$bc41056$,$bc41057$large_gap_both_discontent$bc41057$,$bc41058$Case dismissed. Ritual now official.$bc41058$,$bc41059$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance.$bc41059$,$bc41060$You've each been quietly grading the other's reassurance style as 'too much' or 'not enough' without ever agreeing on what's actually needed here. This was never a mismatch of love, just of translation. Agreeing on one shared ritual turns two mismatched translations into a language you're both fluent in.$bc41060$,$bc41061$say your real need, out loud, no minimizing it. same.$bc41061$,$bc41062$Case dismissed. Ritual now official.$bc41062$,$bc41063$Approved v4 template$bc41063$,$bc41064$Shared Discovery$bc41064$,$bc41065$[A] = the partner who needs more reassurance.$bc41065$,$bc41066$say your real need, out loud, no minimizing it.$bc41066$,$bc41067$same.$bc41067$,$bc41068$Land on one shared 'reassurance ritual' you both agree to use, whatever it is, and actually use it after your next rough patch.$bc41068$,$bc41069$Case dismissed. Ritual now official.$bc41069$),
